{-
   A heap whose allocations are recognizable from the address alone - Design-Allocator.md §5's
   address classification, and the provider that makes `known.def`'s `isHeapAddress` a filled role.

   Selected by `-allocator window` (`# allocator: window` in a fixture). The one beside it in
   `Heap.native.yana` is `bootstrap`, and the two are here to be *different policies* rather than the
   same policy annotated twice: this one gives up the region chain in exchange for a base and a limit
   that two comparisons answer.

   # What the compiler does with it

   A boxed field's storage has to be handed back where a value dies, and the release site cannot
   always see where the value was placed - an overwrite through a `&` borrow is given an address and
   a type and nothing about the caller's frame. A build whose provider answers `isHeapAddress` asks
   the address; a build whose provider does not makes every record with a `@box` carry one hidden bit
   per box saying whether that allocation is the program's. So selecting this provider takes a word
   out of those records, which is what `test/resolve/ownership/Box.Classified.yana` asserts against
   `Box.Stored.yana` beside it.

   # The policy

   **One region, taken once, and never grown.** Bump from the front, and a free list per size class
   threaded through the payloads exactly as the bootstrap heap does - the class table and the header
   word are the same arrangement, because what this provider is demonstrating is the *classification*
   and not a better allocator.

   Not growing is the whole of what buys the two-comparison answer, and it is an honest simplification
   rather than a hidden one: a real provider of this kind reserves a large aligned window up front and
   commits pages inside it as they are wanted, so the base and the limit are still one pair and the
   classification is still a mask. What that needs and this has not got is a distinction between
   reserving address space and committing it, which is a second `mmap` protection and a third syscall
   `Native` does not declare. A program that exhausts the region gets a null from `allocateHeap`,
   which is the same answer the bootstrap heap gives when the kernel refuses it.

   **Nothing but a build that names it reads this file**, which is what `@allocator(window)` on every
   declaration means. It came here from `prelude/` when that library was retired (J21); it is the only
   second provider the compiler has, and it is the only reason the classified release mode is
   reachable at all.
-}

-- 64 MiB, taken in one mapping. Larger than the bootstrap heap's first region because there is no
-- second one to fall back to, and small enough that a test program mapping it costs nothing: the
-- pages are untouched until something is allocated in them.
@allocator(window) pub let heapWindowSize = 67108864 :: Size
@allocator(window) pub let heapWindowClasses = 32 :: Size

-- The window, and the bump pointer inside it. `heapWindowBase` is what `isHeapAddress` compares
-- against and is written exactly once, which is what makes the comparison answer about the whole
-- program's heap rather than about whichever region ran most recently.
@allocator(window) pub let &heapWindowBase = 0 :: %U8
@allocator(window) pub let &heapWindowNext = 0 :: %U8
@allocator(window) pub let &heapWindowLimit = 0 :: %U8
@allocator(window) pub let &heapWindowFree = 0 :: %(%U8)

{-
   The window, mapped once in the life of a program.

   `@noinline` on `Heap.native.yana`'s `initHeap` reasoning exactly: this runs once and
   `allocateHeap` runs at every allocation, and a body with one caller is one the inliner moves
   rather than copies - which costs no bytes and still puts a syscall in front of the bump pointer.

   `@cold` beside it says the same thing about every copy in the other direction - see `initHeap`,
   which carries the pair for the same reason.
-}
@allocator(window) @noinline @cold
fn openWindow():
    -- One page beyond the limit, for the tail-read guarantee the bootstrap heap states: a vector
    -- loop's last iteration reads a whole register where only part of it is wanted, and storage the
    -- language allocated has room after it.
    let region = mapMemory(heapWindowSize + pageBytes)
    if isNull(region) then return

    let table = heapWindowClasses * 8
    setMemory(region, 0, table)

    heapWindowFree = bitcast(region) :: %(%U8)
    heapWindowBase = region
    heapWindowNext = region + table
    heapWindowLimit = region + heapWindowSize

@allocator(window)
fn windowClassOf(total: Size) -> Size:
    let &size = 16 :: Size
    let &sizeClass = 0 :: Size

    while size < total:
        size = size + size
        sizeClass = sizeClass + 1

    return sizeClass

@allocator(window)
fn windowFreeHead(sizeClass: Size) -> %U8 = *(heapWindowFree + sizeClass)

@allocator(window)
fn setWindowFreeHead(sizeClass: Size, block: %U8):
    store(heapWindowFree + sizeClass, block)

{-
   Allocates `size` bytes inside the window, 8-byte aligned, or null when the window is exhausted or
   the request is larger than the largest size class.

   The header word in front of every payload holds the size class, so `freeHeap` needs no table and
   no search - the same arrangement the bootstrap heap uses, and the reason every block is at least
   sixteen bytes.
-}
@allocator(window) @builtin(allocateHeap)
pub fn allocateHeap(size: Size) -> %U8:
    if isNull(heapWindowFree):
        openWindow()
        if isNull(heapWindowFree) then return null()

    let sizeClass = windowClassOf(size + 8)
    if sizeClass >= heapWindowClasses then return null()

    let reused = windowFreeHead(sizeClass)
    if !isNull(reused):
        setWindowFreeHead(sizeClass, *(bitcast(reused) :: %(%U8)))
        return reused

    let blockSize = 16 `shl` sizeClass
    if heapWindowNext + blockSize > heapWindowLimit then return null()

    let block = heapWindowNext
    heapWindowNext = block + blockSize
    store(bitcast(block) :: %Size, sizeClass)

    return block + 8

@allocator(window) @builtin(freeHeap)
pub fn freeHeap(allocation: %U8):
    if isNull(allocation) then return

    let sizeClass = *(bitcast(allocation - 8) :: %Size)

    store(bitcast(allocation) :: %(%U8), windowFreeHead(sizeClass))
    setWindowFreeHead(sizeClass, allocation)

{-
   Whether an address is inside this provider's window - `known.def`'s `isHeapAddress`, and the whole
   reason this provider exists.

   Two comparisons against a pair of words, which is what a single reserved region buys. A provider
   that took several would answer the same question with a mask against an aligned window or a lookup
   in a sparse page directory; what none of them may do is guess, because the compiler's *release*
   path is the caller - an address this answers `True` for is one `freeHeap` is then handed.

   `False` before the window is opened, which is not a special case: nothing has been allocated, so
   no address the program is holding came from here.
-}
@allocator(window) @builtin(isHeapAddress)
pub fn isHeapAddress(address: %U8) -> Bool:
    if isNull(heapWindowBase) then return False
    if address < heapWindowBase then return False
    return address < heapWindowLimit
